# The origin this deployment publishes: canonical, social, and later sitemap URLs are built from it, and a release
# build refuses to run without a valid value (`scripts/check-site-url/check-site-url.ts`). It is public configuration, never a secret,
# and it is never derived from a request's Host header. A prerendered page bakes the build-time value into its HTML, so
# the deployed frontend must carry that same public value. GitLab owns the stage value in both `stage-build` and
# `stage`; its exact runtime variable table is in docs/engineering/ops/deployment.md. Stage uses
# https://stage.caniupdate.dev, while production uses https://caniupdate.dev. Plain http is accepted only for an
# address that is this machine's own — localhost or a LAN/Tailscale IP literal — so a page opened from another device renders matching canonical links:
# NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://100.122.224.20:5173

# Stage CI supplies NUXT_BUILD_COMMIT_SHA (full source SHA) and NUXT_BUILD_REPOSITORY_URL (HTTPS GitLab project URL)
# together at build time. They are public, compiled artifact identity, not runtime settings. Leave both unset for
# ordinary local builds; setting only one fails configuration. Never use a mutable branch name or copy CI secrets here.

# The Nuxt Worker owns no AI setting, no provider credential, and no D1 binding. A briefing submission crosses the
# private `BRIEFING_SERVICE` binding to the briefing Worker, which reads local settings from the git-ignored
# `workers/briefing/.dev.vars` beside its Wrangler config. For stage, GitLab owns the runtime inputs: the public site
# URL is scoped to `stage-build` and `stage`, while every other input is scoped only to `stage`. Deployment derives
# each Worker's runtime values and passes them through a temporary `--secrets-file`; no stage runtime values live in
# Wrangler `env.preview.vars` or the Cloudflare dashboard alone. Nuxt still reads this `.env`, but no worker settings
# below belong to it locally, and nothing is copied into `.output/server/.dev.vars`.

# Local fixture: point the three briefing-Worker settings at a loopback OpenAI-compatible endpoint and treat its output
# as a test double rather than inference. The values live in `workers/briefing/.dev.vars`, not here:
#   AI_BRIEFING_ENDPOINT="http://127.0.0.1:8787/v1/chat/completions"
#   AI_BRIEFING_API_KEY="local-fixture-key"
#   AI_BRIEFING_MODEL="local-fixture-model"

# Stage requires non-empty values for the same three briefing-Worker settings; GitLab supplies them as briefing Worker
# secrets (use a separate stage key). The endpoint is the complete HTTPS OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions URL: its
# path must end with /chat/completions; URL credentials, query parameters, and fragments are rejected.
#   AI_BRIEFING_ENDPOINT=https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/chat/completions
#   AI_BRIEFING_API_KEY=<key>
#   AI_BRIEFING_MODEL=deepseek-v4-flash
# OpenRouter alternative: https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions with deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash.

# Evidence provider credentials belong to the private evidence Worker: an optional GITHUB_TOKEN goes in
# `workers/evidence/.dev.vars` for local runs; stage receives it as an evidence Worker secret from GitLab. Do not copy
# AI credentials there, and do not put a real provider key in this file.

# Optional logs + traces export directly to Grafana Cloud over OTLP/HTTP JSON; no container or Collector.
# Local frontend settings use the frontend server's private runtime config:
#   NUXT_OTEL_ENDPOINT=https://otlp-gateway-<region>.grafana.net/otlp
#   NUXT_PUBLIC_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=true
#   NUXT_OTEL_HEADERS={"authorization":"Basic <base64-instanceId:token>"}
# These NUXT_OTEL_* names are local frontend inputs. The briefing Worker's separate local inputs keep their
# OTEL_EXPORTER_* names in workers/briefing/.dev.vars (example below).
# For GitLab stage, use a single protected `stage`-scoped input pair: OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS. CI reads and validates the JSON string-to-string header map once, then derives the
# frontend NUXT_OTEL_* and briefing OTEL_EXPORTER_* Worker runtime values from that pair. Only those two Workers
# receive the shared CI inputs; the evidence Worker has independent private configuration, shown below.
# Both shared inputs are optional. When omitted, CI sends each managed runtime an empty endpoint and `{}` headers,
# clearing previously managed values. NUXT_PUBLIC_TELEMETRY_ENABLED remains a required stage `true`/`false` value:
# true requires the shared endpoint and controls frontend export only; the briefing Worker exports whenever its
# derived endpoint is configured. GitLab stage endpoints must use HTTPS and have no URL credentials, query, or
# fragment; local development may use a loopback HTTP endpoint as shown below.
# Encrypting the public NUXT_PUBLIC_* values does not make them private. Never put real credentials in this example file.
# The application-side privacy policy sanitizes records before export; console diagnostics use the same field-filtered allowlist.
# Header values are single-line JSON objects whose keys and values are strings. Browser logs/traces go through same-origin
# /api/telemetry/v1/{logs,traces}; never place endpoints or authentication headers in NUXT_PUBLIC_* configuration.
# The briefing Worker's local socket vars remain ws://localhost:8791 and http://localhost:5173 in its Wrangler config.
# Locally, BRIEFING_ALLOWED_ORIGIN remains a briefing Wrangler `vars` setting. For stage, GitLab supplies
# BRIEFING_PUBLIC_URL=wss://stage-briefing.caniupdate.dev and deployment derives BRIEFING_ALLOWED_ORIGIN from
# NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL (https://stage.caniupdate.dev); do not add a GitLab BRIEFING_ALLOWED_ORIGIN input. This exact
# origin allowlist remains a security control. The briefing Worker's local telemetry settings in
# workers/briefing/.dev.vars are:
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS={"authorization":"Basic <instanceId:token>"}
# The evidence Worker owns independent optional OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS inputs
# in workers/evidence/.dev.vars. Do not copy these credentials into Nuxt public config or assume stage CI configures them.
# Unconfigured runtimes retain the same field-filtered console diagnostics without remote export.
